1038
00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:02.710
Because I'm, there is no way I would have done that.
因为我，我绝对不可能去做那种事。

1039
00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:03.290
No way.
绝对不可能。

1040
00:51:03.650 --> 00:51:05.370
I am too much of a scaredy cat.
我胆子太小了。

1041
00:51:06.270 --> 00:51:09.710
You know, what's interesting is that one of the things I really wanted to do on this show,
你知道，有趣的是，我在这个剧里真正想做的事情之一，

1042
00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:10.490
I don't know why.
我也不知道为什么。

1043
00:51:10.630 --> 00:51:12.470
I think, I think I'm a badass driver.
我想，我觉得自己是个车技高手。

1044
00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:13.630
I'm probably not.
可能我并不是。

1045
00:51:13.790 --> 00:51:16.110
But I always wanted to do all my driving stunts.
但我一直想亲自完成所有的驾驶特技。

1046
00:51:16.230 --> 00:51:17.930
And when they let me, like, because, you know,
当他们允许我这么做的时候，因为，你知道，

1047
00:51:17.990 --> 00:51:19.810
Dwight and I have a few driving scenes.
我和德怀特有几场驾驶戏。

1048
00:51:20.050 --> 00:51:21.330
I would get really excited.
我会感到非常兴奋。

1049
00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:22.810
Don't want to do those either.
那些我也不想做。

1050
00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:25.890
I like it when they hook it up to a rig and pull the car.
我更喜欢他们把车挂在拖车架上拉着走。

1051
00:51:26.090 --> 00:51:28.570
I'm like, I don't want to drive this down the road while I'm acting.
我就想，我可不想在演戏的时候还要开车。

1052
00:51:28.830 --> 00:51:29.910
No one wants that.
没人想那样。

1053
00:51:30.090 --> 00:51:31.210
I've had to do that before.
我以前不得不那样做过。

1054
00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:32.490
I'm always terrified.
我总是很害怕。

1055
00:51:34.230 --> 00:51:39.710
Well, one thing that did make it into the scene is this very odd moment of Daryl finding his iPod.
嗯，有一件事确实出现在了正片里，就是达里尔发现他那个iPod的奇怪瞬间。

1056
00:51:40.150 --> 00:51:42.650
And I want you to know that is not in the script.
我想让你们知道，剧本里并没有这一段。

1057
00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:45.350
That was an improvisation by Craig on the day.
那是克雷格当天即兴发挥的。

1058
00:51:45.490 --> 00:51:51.730
He actually found an iPod deck and speaker somewhere back in the warehouse.
他其实是在仓库后面的某个地方找到了一个iPod底座和扬声器。

1059
00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.630
And he just did this bit and it made it into the show.
他随手演了这一出，结果就被剪进剧里了。

1060
00:51:54.730 --> 00:51:56.170
And it is so funny to me.
我觉得这太搞笑了。

1061
00:51:56.410 --> 00:51:58.210
We got so much mail about it.
我们收到了好多关于这事的来信。

1062
00:51:58.310 --> 00:52:00.370
People were like, is there something I'm missing?
大家都在问，我是不是错过了什么？

1063
00:52:00.370 --> 00:52:06.050
Is there a deleted scene from a previous episode where Daryl loses his iPod?
是不是之前的剧集里有删减片段，讲的是达里尔弄丢了iPod？

1064
00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:09.050
Is this product placement for iPod again?
这又是iPod的植入广告吗？

1065
00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:11.710
No, it was just this random funny bit.
不，这只是个随意的搞笑片段。

1066
00:52:11.910 --> 00:52:15.830
He does it with such, like, real enthusiasm.
他演得特别投入，那种热情特别真实。

1067
00:52:16.510 --> 00:52:20.570
Well, we had to be there pretending like we were going through boxes, guys.
其实当时我们得在那儿假装翻箱倒柜，伙计们。

1068
00:52:20.570 --> 00:52:24.550
So we were really doing stuff, which you don't really see.
所以我们是真的在干活，虽然你们没怎么看到。

1069
00:52:24.630 --> 00:52:26.030
It didn't really make it into the show.
那些镜头没怎么剪进正片里。

1070
00:52:26.110 --> 00:52:31.630
But I think that he legitimately opened up a box and that was in there and he got all excited.
但我认为他当时是真打开了一个箱子，里面正好有那个东西，他就特别兴奋。

1071
00:52:31.630 --> 00:52:33.530
Yes, exactly.
没错，就是这样。

1072
00:52:34.030 --> 00:52:38.910
Well, I have a little Angela Roy moment that happens right at the same time.
嗯，我有一个小小的安吉拉和罗伊的瞬间，正好发生在同一时间。

1073
00:52:39.130 --> 00:52:48.010
At 14 minutes, 44 seconds, you can see Angela starts to walk up to Roy and is about to, like, go up to him.
在14分44秒的时候，你可以看到安吉拉开始走向罗伊，正准备去找他。

1074
00:52:48.130 --> 00:52:51.430
And then Daryl yells, I found an iPod dock.
然后达里尔大喊：我找到了一个iPod底座。

1075
00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:54.070
And I whip my head and I pivot the other way.
我猛地一回头，转了个身往另一个方向走了。

1076
00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:54.630
Wow.
哇。

1077
00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:56.650
You have to go and watch it.
你们一定要去看看。

1078
00:52:56.930 --> 00:53:00.090
More of the untold Angela Roy story.
更多安吉拉和罗伊不为人知的故事。

1079
00:53:00.090 --> 00:53:04.190
And I'm wondering, I mean, what was meant to happen there?
我在想，我是说，那里本来应该发生什么？

1080
00:53:04.310 --> 00:53:05.330
Like, it's not in the script.
剧本里没写。

1081
00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:06.470
It's not in deleted scenes.
删减片段里也没有。

1082
00:53:06.790 --> 00:53:12.530
But I'm sure I was given some direction maybe to go over and, you know, in the background sort of talk to Roy.
但我肯定当时有人指导我过去，在背景里和罗伊聊聊天什么的。

1083
00:53:13.250 --> 00:53:16.470
But yeah, when Daryl yells, I turn around and go the other direction.
但确实，当达里尔大喊时，我就转身往另一个方向走了。

1084
00:53:16.730 --> 00:53:17.830
It's a fun little catch.
这是一个很有趣的小细节。

1085
00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:26.030
Well, then we see Pam is doing some inventory and Karen comes up to her and thanks her.
接下来我们看到帕姆在做库存盘点，凯伦走过来感谢她。

1086
00:53:26.030 --> 00:53:30.550
She says, thank you so much for talking sense into Jim.
她说，太感谢你劝通吉姆了。

1087
00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:32.850
Karen is so happy.
凯伦特别开心。

1088
00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:35.390
Her relationship is back on.
她的感情生活又恢复正常了。

1089
00:53:35.610 --> 00:53:36.490
Everything's okay.
一切都好起来了。

1090
00:53:36.990 --> 00:53:42.890
And then we cut to Pam just sobbing on a bench in the hallway.
然后镜头一转，帕姆正坐在走廊的长椅上啜泣。

1091
00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:54.700
And Angela, I watched this scene and it just brought tears to my eyes because I remember shooting it so clearly, so clearly.
安吉拉，我看了这一幕，眼泪都掉下来了，因为我记得当时拍摄的情景，记得特别清楚，特别清楚。

1092
00:53:54.740 --> 00:53:58.320
And we got a lot of questions about it.
我们收到了很多关于这一幕的问题。

1093
00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:06.940
So we got questions from Joe Salazar, Angie Letworth, Elisa Goldman, Jillian Reilly, and Charlie Sparkenbaugh.
我们收到了来自乔·萨拉查、安吉·莱特沃斯、埃莉萨·戈德曼、吉莉安·赖利和查理·斯帕肯鲍的提问。

1094
00:54:07.200 --> 00:54:12.500
They wanted to know, how was it filming that iconic Pam and Dwight scene when Pam is crying?
他们想知道，拍摄那个经典的帕姆和德怀特的哭戏是什么感觉？

1095
00:54:13.520 --> 00:54:16.800
Well, guys, this was like early on in the show.
嗯，伙计们，那是剧集早期的时候。

1096
00:54:17.020 --> 00:54:26.640
And whenever we had these really emotional scenes, like that parking lot scene with Jim and Pam, we would just kind of shut down everything.
每当我们有这种非常情绪化的戏份时，比如吉姆和帕姆在停车场那场戏，我们就会把一切都停下来。

1097
00:54:26.860 --> 00:54:34.400
And I remember that we shut down the set and we took hours prepping and shooting this crying scene.
我记得当时我们清空了片场，花了几个小时来准备和拍摄这场哭戏。

1098
00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:37.940
It was really important to Greg that I really be crying.
格雷格非常看重我是否真的在哭。

1099
00:54:38.600 --> 00:54:41.220
They did not want to put fake tears in my eyes.
他们不想在我的眼睛里滴眼药水。

1100
00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:42.880
He wanted me to really go there.
他希望我能真正进入那种状态。

1101
00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:46.380
And this is a really big challenge for an actor.
这对演员来说是一个巨大的挑战。

1102
00:54:47.680 --> 00:54:51.360
And so what they did was they made everything really quiet.
所以他们所做的就是让周围变得非常安静。

1103
00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:54.760
And no one talked to me for about a half hour.
大约半小时里没有人跟我说话。

1104
00:54:54.880 --> 00:54:57.920
And I put on the saddest music I could find.
我放了我能找到的最悲伤的音乐。

1105
00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:02.700
And it was sad because it was personal to me, right?
那真的很伤感，因为那对我个人而言意义非凡，对吧？

1106
00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:13.820
It was music that brought up memories of breakups, music that I listened to at different times in my life when I was having a particularly hard time.
那些音乐勾起了我失恋的回忆，是我人生中在特别艰难的时期听过的歌。

1107
00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:16.240
So it would bring up a lot of emotions.
所以它会引发很多情绪。

1108
00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:23.580
And I had spent the previous week creating this playlist because I knew that it would invoke a lot of emotion.
我花了一周时间制作了这个播放列表，因为我知道它会激发很多情感。

1109
00:55:24.100 --> 00:55:27.060
And I sat and I listened to it until I was crying.
我坐在那儿一直听，直到我哭出来。

1110
00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:32.160
And then Kelly, our first AD, came and took the headphones away from me.
然后我们的第一副导演凯利走过来，拿走了我的耳机。

1111
00:55:32.560 --> 00:55:34.140
And then they started rolling.
接着他们就开始拍摄了。

1112
00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:38.800
And that's how they were able to begin the scene with me crying.
这就是他们能让我以哭戏开场的原因。

1113
00:55:39.580 --> 00:55:44.460
Because when you play a scene, Angela, and you have dialogue and you have connection with a person,
因为安吉拉，当你演一场戏时，如果你有台词，并且与对方有交流，

1114
00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:48.220
a lot of times that will bring the emotion out of you.
很多时候这会激发你的情绪。

1115
00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:53.700
But it's really hard to start a scene in a full cry because you would just have to get yourself there.
但要在开场时就大哭真的很难，因为你必须强迫自己进入那种状态。

1116
00:55:54.740 --> 00:55:55.980
No, that's so true.
不，你说得太对了。

1117
00:55:56.160 --> 00:56:00.440
It's actually really hard because you're basically taking yourself emotionally from zero to 60.
这其实非常难，因为你基本上是在情感上强行从零加速到一百。

1118
00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:06.400
And I had to do a crying scene in a show called Haters Back Off that was like something in the middle.
我曾在一部叫《喷子滚开》的剧里演过一场哭戏，大概是在剧情中间的部分。

1119
00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:12.040
And I remember they let me sort of just step away from set to get myself there.
我记得他们让我离开片场一会儿，好让我自己酝酿情绪。

1120
00:56:12.160 --> 00:56:14.100
And once I was there, they started rolling.
等我进入状态后，他们就开始拍摄了。

1121
00:56:14.320 --> 00:56:17.900
I took this acting class and it was talking about emotional journeys.
我上过一个表演课，里面讲到了情感历程。

1122
00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:25.700
And the acting teacher encouraged us to pick a song, one song, because sometimes on set,
表演老师鼓励我们选一首歌，只选一首，因为有时候在片场，

1123
00:56:25.820 --> 00:56:28.980
you don't have the luxury of being able to like go listen.
你没有那种奢侈的时间去慢慢听歌。

1124
00:56:28.980 --> 00:56:33.460
That really shows how much our show was taking care of you in that moment, I feel like.
我觉得这真的体现了当时我们的剧组是多么照顾你。

1125
00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:39.980
But sometimes you only have a few minutes to reset yourself emotionally and to get to that place.
但有时你只有几分钟时间来调整情绪，让自己进入状态。

1126
00:56:40.220 --> 00:56:42.860
So we took, everyone picked one song.
所以我们每个人都选了一首歌。

1127
00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:45.760
And in your mind, you sing that one song.
在脑海里，你跟着唱那首歌。

1128
00:56:45.880 --> 00:56:49.840
And by the end of that song, you should be in that emotional place.
等到歌唱完的时候，你就应该进入那种情感状态了。

1129
00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:52.200
And it really worked for me.
这对我很管用。

1130
00:56:52.400 --> 00:56:56.160
And I find for myself now, I'm older now, guys.
我现在发现，伙计们，我年纪大了。

1131
00:56:56.160 --> 00:57:00.280
I'm 49 and more life has happened to me.
我已经49岁了，经历过更多的人生起伏。

1132
00:57:00.800 --> 00:57:06.420
And it's easier for me to get to that place than when I was a younger actor.
比起年轻时，现在我更容易进入那种状态了。

1133
00:57:06.940 --> 00:57:07.420
Yeah, same.
是啊，我也是。

1134
00:57:08.060 --> 00:57:11.980
But music is a big way to trigger it for me.
但音乐对我来说是触发情绪的重要方式。

1135
00:57:12.100 --> 00:57:13.780
And I think for a lot of actors.
我想对很多演员来说也是如此。

1136
00:57:14.100 --> 00:57:17.860
I've created musical soundtracks for a lot of things I've done.
我为我做过的很多作品都制作过音乐原声带。

1137
00:57:18.100 --> 00:57:21.940
I had to do a play and I had to start out the play in a rage.
我曾演过一部戏，开场时我必须处于愤怒的状态。

1138
00:57:21.940 --> 00:57:24.120
I had to start out very, very angry.
我必须一上来就非常、非常生气。

1139
00:57:24.120 --> 00:57:28.260
And I couldn't find a song that exactly triggered my anger.
但我找不到一首歌能精准地触发我的愤怒。

1140
00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:30.200
I had a song that would kind of amp me up.
我有一首歌能让我稍微兴奋起来。

1141
00:57:30.280 --> 00:57:40.220
And then I realized I had some audio of an ex-boyfriend who I really was pissed at still to this day.
后来我意识到，我还有一段前男友的录音，直到今天我想到他还是很生气。

1142
00:57:40.620 --> 00:57:44.480
I found this audio tape and I played his voice.
我找到了那盘录音带，播放了他的声音。

1143
00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:49.720
And it made me so damn mad that I would, you can hear me right now just thinking about it.
那真的让我火冒三丈，你现在光听我提起这件事就能听出来。

1144
00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:58.020
It made me so damn mad that I could just walk out on stage and start screaming at the other actor on stage because just his voice triggered me.
那让我气到可以直接走上舞台，对着另一位演员大吼大叫，因为光是他的声音就足以触发我。

1145
00:57:58.420 --> 00:57:58.520
Wow.
哇。

1146
00:57:58.580 --> 00:58:02.200
So I think that, you know, music, something you can listen to.
所以我认为，你知道，音乐，或者任何你可以听的东西。

1147
00:58:03.700 --> 00:58:04.100
Yeah.
没错。

1148
00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:05.000
That works.
那很有效。

1149
00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:06.560
Yeah, I think that goes.
是啊，我觉得是这样。

1150
00:58:06.700 --> 00:58:08.240
I mean, you guys listening, you can.
我是说，你们这些听众，你们也可以。

1151
00:58:08.400 --> 00:58:11.980
I know there are songs that take you right back to a moment in your life.
我知道有些歌能让你瞬间回到生命中的某个时刻。

1152
00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:14.660
That's that's the power and beauty of music, right?
这就是音乐的力量和美妙之处，对吧？

1153
00:58:14.860 --> 00:58:22.100
The song that like would make me tear up by the end is a lefty Feazell song called That's the Way Love Goes.
有一首歌每次听完都会让我热泪盈眶，那是Lefty Frizzell唱的《That's the Way Love Goes》。

1154
00:58:24.280 --> 00:58:26.460
So and it's an old song.
那是一首老歌。

1155
00:58:26.620 --> 00:58:27.440
Some of you will know it.
你们有些人可能听过。

1156
00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:33.100
Some of you in this moment know that I'm 100 years old because it's a really old song.
你们有些人现在肯定觉得我有一百岁了，因为这歌确实很老。

1157
00:58:33.100 --> 00:58:38.100
Well, one of the songs I was listening to to help make me cry for Pam was a Ben Fold song,
嗯，我当时为了让自己能为帕姆（Pam）那场戏哭出来，听的歌里有一首本·福兹（Ben Folds）的歌，

1158
00:58:38.140 --> 00:58:46.640
but I had a whole Pam soundtrack that I would listen to on my drive to work during season three when her and Jim were estranged.
但我其实有一整套“帕姆歌单”，在第三季她和吉姆（Jim）疏远期间，我开车上班时都会听。

1159
00:58:47.060 --> 00:58:49.540
And it was Nora Jones.
里面有诺拉·琼斯（Norah Jones）的歌。

1160
00:58:50.840 --> 00:58:51.440
Lady.
那位女士。

1161
00:58:51.800 --> 00:58:55.320
She's just so soulful and wishing and.
她的歌声太深情了，充满了渴望和……

1162
00:58:56.280 --> 00:58:57.720
A little loSt.
一点点迷茫。

1163
00:58:58.020 --> 00:59:02.600
I remember you guys doing the scene and I remember it from the filter of your BFF.
我记得你们拍那场戏的时候，我是以你闺蜜的视角来看待的。

1164
00:59:03.100 --> 00:59:05.220
And I remember you were really anxious about it.
我记得你当时特别焦虑。

1165
00:59:05.300 --> 00:59:06.800
You really wanted to get it right.
你真的很想把那场戏演好。

1166
00:59:07.120 --> 00:59:08.700
You were doing all the prep.
你做了所有的准备工作。

1167
00:59:08.900 --> 00:59:12.860
I remember when you guys went to do the scene that I left you alone.
我记得你们去拍那场戏时，我特意避开了。

1168
00:59:13.240 --> 00:59:16.660
You know, you don't need your chatty five foot one.
你知道的，你不需要我这个一米五五的唠叨鬼在旁边。

1169
00:59:18.700 --> 00:59:21.260
I was like, I'm going to give her all the space she needs.
我当时想，我要给她留出她所需要的所有空间。

1170
00:59:21.660 --> 00:59:23.940
And rewatching it, Jenna, it made me tear up.
珍娜（Jenna），重看那场戏时，我真的看哭了。

1171
00:59:24.040 --> 00:59:24.880
It's so beautiful.
太美了。

1172
00:59:25.140 --> 00:59:28.700
And I think I see rain as Dwight tearing up.
而且我觉得我看到了雷恩（Rainn）作为德怀特（Dwight）在流泪。

1173
00:59:29.000 --> 00:59:30.920
But surely Dwight wouldn't tear up.
但德怀特肯定不会流泪的。

1174
00:59:30.920 --> 00:59:35.320
But I think rain, your friend, is tearing up watching you cry.
但我认为雷恩，你的朋友，是在看着你哭的时候自己也跟着哭了。

1175
00:59:36.660 --> 00:59:49.080
Angela, it's making me get choked up when you say it because this is a little bit of a place where the line blurred between the rain that you and I know.
安吉拉（Angela），你这么说让我哽咽了，因为这确实模糊了我们认识的雷恩和角色之间的界限。

1176
00:59:49.280 --> 00:59:49.440
Yeah.
没错。

1177
00:59:49.580 --> 00:59:50.120
And Dwight.
还有德怀特。

1178
00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:55.260
Rain simply couldn't do that scene without empathizing with Pam.
雷恩根本无法在不与帕姆共情的情况下完成那场戏。

1179
00:59:55.900 --> 01:00:00.320
Every time we did a take, he teared up when I cried harder.
每次我们拍一条，我哭得越厉害，他就跟着流泪。

1180
01:00:00.680 --> 01:00:07.900
It was like Rain Wilson, the man, was incapable of sitting next to a person in pain without feeling their pain.
就好像雷恩·威尔森（Rainn Wilson）本人，根本无法坐在一个痛苦的人身边而不去感受对方的痛苦。

1181
01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:09.860
And this is the thing that we know about rain.
这就是我们所了解的雷恩。

1182
01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:11.500
It's the thing that we love about rain.
也是我们爱他的原因。

1183
01:00:11.500 --> 01:00:20.220
And when I saw it in the scene, it just made me miss rain so much because that's who rain is to me on a personal level.
当我在戏里看到这一幕时，我真的太想念雷恩了，因为这就是我私下里认识的雷恩。

1184
01:00:20.720 --> 01:00:20.900
Yeah.
是啊。

1185
01:00:21.440 --> 01:00:21.900
Yeah.
没错。

1186
01:00:21.900 --> 01:00:23.100
I just saw rain.
我刚才看到了雷恩。

1187
01:00:24.040 --> 01:00:26.460
I just saw him and he's tearing up.
我刚才看到他了，他正在流泪。

1188
01:00:26.600 --> 01:00:29.180
And then he has to snap back to Dwight.
然后他得立刻切换回德怀特的状态。

1189
01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:29.920
Yeah.
没错。

1190
01:00:29.920 --> 01:00:33.580
You know, and say the line or, you know, PMSing pretty hard, right?
你知道，还得说出那句台词，或者说，“经期综合征挺严重的吧”，对吧？

1191
01:00:33.880 --> 01:00:34.100
Yeah.
是啊。

1192
01:00:34.540 --> 01:00:35.000
Yes.
没错。

1193
01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:43.500
One of my favorite things he does in this scene as Dwight is he takes his jacket off and you're positive he's going to wrap it around Pam.
他在那场戏里作为德怀特做的我最喜欢的动作之一，就是他脱下外套，你肯定以为他要给帕姆披上。

1194
01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:47.600
And he ties it around his waist so awkwardly.
结果他笨拙地把它系在了腰上。

1195
01:00:48.520 --> 01:00:50.260
Yes, that was in the script.
是的，那是剧本里写的。

1196
01:00:50.260 --> 01:00:51.500
That's the other thing.
还有一点。

1197
01:00:51.740 --> 01:01:00.240
All the little beats, all the moments, him putting his jacket around his waist, giving the handkerchief, awkwardly putting his arm around her shoulder.
所有那些细微的节奏，所有的瞬间，他把外套系在腰上，递手帕，笨拙地把手搭在她肩膀上。

1198
01:01:00.500 --> 01:01:01.580
All of that is scripted.
所有这些都是剧本里写好的。

1199
01:01:01.700 --> 01:01:02.400
All those moments.
所有那些时刻。

1200
01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:04.860
It's just it was a beautifully written scene.
那真是一场写得非常精彩的戏。

1201
01:01:04.940 --> 01:01:08.720
And they gave us great time to figure it out and shoot it.
他们给了我们充足的时间去构思和拍摄。

1202
01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:10.260
I'm really proud of that scene.
我真的为那一幕感到自豪。

1203
01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:12.880
It's one of my favorites from the series that I got to do.
那是我在剧集中最喜欢的片段之一。

1204
01:01:13.120 --> 01:01:13.480
I know.
我知道。

1205
01:01:13.620 --> 01:01:15.320
You just did it so well.
你演得太棒了。

1206
01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:16.700
Where are we now?
我们现在到哪儿了？

1207
01:01:17.460 --> 01:01:19.100
Well, Jan has arrived.
嗯，简到了。

1208
01:01:19.100 --> 01:01:21.180
Oh, it's so awkward.
哦，太尴尬了。

1209
01:01:21.700 --> 01:01:23.100
Jan has arrived.
简到了。

1210
01:01:23.200 --> 01:01:28.340
And you could you could cut the tension, even though we were acting.
那种紧张感简直触手可及，尽管我们只是在演戏。

1211
01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:31.500
I remember Melora has such a presence.
我记得梅洛拉的气场非常强大。

1212
01:01:31.720 --> 01:01:38.800
And when she walked into that warehouse, it was like you could hear a pin drop, even though we knew we were acting.
当她走进那个仓库时，简直落针可闻，尽管我们知道是在演戏。

1213
01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:40.460
It just was a powerful moment.
那真是一个震撼的时刻。

1214
01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:42.160
Yes.
没错。

1215
01:01:42.420 --> 01:01:48.680
And Michael's slow walk up the stairs was so good.
还有迈克尔慢悠悠走上楼梯的那段，演得真好。

1216
01:01:49.340 --> 01:01:50.460
So good.
太好了。

1217
01:01:50.840 --> 01:01:51.720
So good.
太好了。

1218
01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:56.420
And then, Jenna, when they get back to Michael's office, what a power move.
然后，珍娜，当他们回到迈克尔办公室时，那招反客为主真是绝了。

1219
01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:58.920
Jan is sitting in his seat.
简坐在了他的位置上。

1220
01:01:59.200 --> 01:02:05.260
He has to sit across from her where the other employees would normally sit.
他不得不坐在她对面，也就是平时员工坐的地方。

1221
01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:06.220
It was so badass.
真是太霸气了。

1222
01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:07.360
I so loved it.
我太喜欢了。

1223
01:02:07.540 --> 01:02:12.040
So now in this scene, you don't know what's going to happen.
现在这一幕，你根本不知道接下来会发生什么。

1224
01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:19.040
You're pretty sure that she knows about this picture and she has come to just lay into Michael about sending out this photo.
你很确定她知道那张照片的事，她是来找迈克尔算账的，因为他发了那张照片。

1225
01:02:19.040 --> 01:02:29.060
But she's either unaware of the photo or she doesn't care because she tells Michael this amazing speech.
但她要么是不知道照片的事，要么就是根本不在乎，因为她对迈克尔发表了一番惊人的演讲。

1226
01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:30.180
It's so amazing.
太惊人了。

1227
01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:31.980
It's so good.
太棒了。

1228
01:02:31.980 --> 01:02:38.560
She says her therapist thinks she has self-destructive tendencies and she should just lean into them.
她说她的心理医生认为她有自我毁灭倾向，她应该顺其自然。

1229
01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:40.960
Let's try that for a change.
让我们换个方式试试。

1230
01:02:41.460 --> 01:02:43.860
Let's just go with the destruction.
我们就顺着这种毁灭的冲动来吧。

1231
01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:45.340
I love it.
我喜欢。

1232
01:02:45.480 --> 01:02:52.080
She says, in this past year, I have been through a divorce, an identity theft, an identity theft.
她说，在过去的一年里，我经历了离婚、身份被盗，身份被盗。

1233
01:02:52.300 --> 01:02:58.620
She's like, my ex-husband, you know, can't or won't share his feelings with me.
她说，我的前夫，你知道，不能或者不愿与我分享他的感受。

1234
01:02:58.620 --> 01:03:08.060
And then she says, maybe she will find happiness by lowering her expectations and following her instinct to be with Michael.
然后她说，也许她可以通过降低期望，追随与迈克尔在一起的直觉来找到幸福。

1235
01:03:08.440 --> 01:03:12.060
Everything in Malora's performance is perfection.
梅洛拉表演中的每一个细节都堪称完美。

1236
01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:18.620
And then it is mirrored by Steve's perfect reactions to everything she's saying.
而史蒂夫对她所说的一切做出的完美反应，正好与之呼应。

1237
01:03:18.740 --> 01:03:19.940
He is confused.
他很困惑。

1238
01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:22.880
He isn't sure if he can be happy.
他不确定自己是否能感到幸福。

1239
01:03:23.020 --> 01:03:25.400
He's clearly, like, afraid to react.
他显然是害怕做出反应。

1240
01:03:25.900 --> 01:03:28.860
Yeah, he's frozen because he's like, she's breaking up with me.
没错，他僵住了，因为他在想，她这是要跟我分手吗？

1241
01:03:29.000 --> 01:03:29.540
Wait, she's not?
等等，她不是吗？

1242
01:03:29.740 --> 01:03:30.680
Which she likes me.
也就是说她喜欢我？

1243
01:03:31.000 --> 01:03:31.800
Oh, but she's leaving me.
哦，但她要离开我了。

1244
01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:32.860
Who's this therapist?
这心理医生是谁？

1245
01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:33.740
What's happening?
到底发生了什么？

1246
01:03:35.060 --> 01:03:36.760
All of those things are happening.
所有这些想法都在他脑子里闪过。

1247
01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:42.240
And then, you know, she grabs him and gives him a big sloppy kiss.
然后，你知道，她抓住他，给了他一个又大又湿的吻。

1248
01:03:42.440 --> 01:03:45.960
I wrote on my card, Jan, hashtag DGAF.
我在卡片上写道：简，标签：根本不在乎。

1249
01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:48.160
What's DGAF?
什么是根本不在乎？

1250
01:03:48.320 --> 01:03:49.720
Don't give a f***.
就是去他妈的，什么都不在乎。

1251
01:03:50.620 --> 01:03:55.080
Oh, because she knows the camera crew is there.
噢，因为她知道摄制组在那儿。

1252
01:03:55.080 --> 01:03:57.320
She is clearly over it.
她显然已经受够了。

1253
01:03:57.520 --> 01:04:00.860
She's like, I'm going to lean into this, just like my doctor said.
她心想，我要顺其自然，就像我医生说的那样。

1254
01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:03.820
She says, meet me back at your condo in 15 minutes.
她说，15分钟后在你的公寓见。

1255
01:04:04.120 --> 01:04:06.920
And then it's my favorite moment.
然后就是我最喜欢的时刻了。

1256
01:04:07.260 --> 01:04:10.540
I mean, well, there's many favorite moments, but this is one of my favorite moments.
我是说，好吧，有很多喜欢的时刻，但这绝对是其中之一。

1257
01:04:11.180 --> 01:04:12.440
He is stunned.
他惊呆了。

1258
01:04:12.980 --> 01:04:16.960
And he looks at her and says, you complete me.
他看着她说，你完整了我。

1259
01:04:17.220 --> 01:04:20.260
Like from Jerry Maguire, like that scene in the elevator.
就像《甜心先生》里电梯里的那一幕一样。

1260
01:04:20.460 --> 01:04:22.000
He's like, you complete me.
他说，你完整了我。

1261
01:04:22.460 --> 01:04:24.200
And she looks at him and pauses.
她看着他，停顿了一下。

1262
01:04:24.200 --> 01:04:26.620
It's such a wonderful pause.
那真是一个美妙的停顿。

1263
01:04:26.620 --> 01:04:28.720
And she goes, oh, God.
然后她说，噢，天哪。

1264
01:04:30.510 --> 01:04:32.610
All of that was scripted, Angela.
那全都是写好的剧本，安吉拉。

1265
01:04:33.010 --> 01:04:34.030
Oh, it's so good.
噢，太棒了。

1266
01:04:34.030 --> 01:04:34.310
All of that.
全都是。

1267
01:04:35.090 --> 01:04:37.130
It says that he says that line.
剧本上写着他说了那句台词。

1268
01:04:37.270 --> 01:04:40.170
And then it says Jan pauses and then sighs.
然后写着简停顿了一下，接着叹了口气。

1269
01:04:41.390 --> 01:04:44.090
But she's like, still going to meet him at his apartment.
但她还是决定去他的公寓见他。

1270
01:04:44.730 --> 01:04:44.870
Yeah.
没错。

1271
01:04:45.070 --> 01:04:47.870
That, oh, God is like, well, I'm in it now.
那句“噢，天哪”就像是在说，好吧，我现在只能硬着头皮上了。

1272
01:04:50.080 --> 01:04:55.020
Lady, we got a fan question about this scene that so intrigued me.
女士，我们收到一个关于这一幕的粉丝提问，非常吸引我。

1273
01:04:55.860 --> 01:04:56.800
Listen to this.
听听这个。

1274
01:04:56.940 --> 01:04:57.080
Okay.
好的。

1275
01:04:57.080 --> 01:04:58.020
This is from Nilly.
这是来自尼利的提问。

1276
01:04:58.540 --> 01:05:06.540
Nilly said, Daniel Levinson and Roger Gould are adult developmental psychologists who have
尼利说，丹尼尔·莱文森和罗杰·古尔德是成人发展心理学家，他们

1277
01:05:06.540 --> 01:05:13.260
contributed massively to the field, including the study and definition of the midlife crisis.
为该领域做出了巨大贡献，包括对中年危机的研究和定义。

1278
01:05:13.860 --> 01:05:19.960
Is Jan Levinson Gould's name a reference to the fact that she is the epitome of a midlife
简·莱文森·古尔德这个名字是否暗示了她是中年

1279
01:05:19.960 --> 01:05:22.340
crisis or is it just a coincidence?
危机的缩影，还是仅仅是个巧合？

1280
01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:25.100
That is an amazing catch.
这真是一个了不起的发现。

1281
01:05:25.240 --> 01:05:30.160
I think it's a coincidence because I don't think they knew that the character of Jan was
我认为这只是个巧合，因为我不认为当我们开始创作时，他们就知道简这个角色

1282
01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:33.460
going to become what she was when we started.
会变成后来那样。

1283
01:05:33.700 --> 01:05:38.480
So when they named her Jan Levinson Gould, they were not aware that she was going to spin
所以当他们给她起名简·莱文森·古尔德时，他们还不知道她会失控

1284
01:05:38.480 --> 01:05:40.800
out and go down this road with Michael yet.
并和迈克尔走上那条路。

1285
01:05:41.060 --> 01:05:42.600
That that happened over time.
那是随着时间推移才发生的。

1286
01:05:42.600 --> 01:05:48.480
But I will tell you that I appreciate this fan letter because it sent me down a fabulous
但我得告诉你，我很感谢这封粉丝来信，因为它让我陷入了一个美妙的

1287
01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:53.840
two hour rabbit hole where I Googled Dr. Roger Gould adult development theory.
两小时深挖之旅，我谷歌了罗杰·古尔德博士的成人发展理论。

1288
01:05:54.240 --> 01:05:54.840
Fascinating.
太迷人了。

1289
01:05:55.520 --> 01:06:01.060
I'd like you to know that you can also get a book called The Seasons of a Man's Life by
我想让你知道，你还可以买到一本丹尼尔·莱文森博士写的书，

1290
01:06:01.060 --> 01:06:02.220
Dr. Daniel Levinson.
叫《男人一生的四季》。

1291
01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:07.360
But all of their work was combined into a wonderful book by Gail Sheehy.
但他们所有的研究都被盖尔·希伊整合进了一本精彩的书里。

1292
01:06:07.540 --> 01:06:12.020
She's a feminist journalist and she wrote passages, predictable crises of adult life.
她是一位女权主义记者，写了《人生转折点：成人生活的可预测危机》一书。

1293
01:06:12.600 --> 01:06:15.180
Did I buy all these books and I'm waiting for them?
我是不是买了所有这些书，正在等它们送达？

1294
01:06:15.580 --> 01:06:16.980
Maybe I did, Nilly.
也许吧，尼利。

1295
01:06:17.240 --> 01:06:17.940
Maybe I did.
也许吧。

1296
01:06:18.560 --> 01:06:19.840
I'll report back.
我会回来汇报的。

1297
01:06:20.100 --> 01:06:23.440
But guys, if you want to go on the journey with me, you can Google it all.
但是伙计们，如果你们想和我一起踏上这段旅程，你们可以去谷歌搜索一下。

1298
01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:24.420
It was really awesome.
这真的很棒。

1299
01:06:25.540 --> 01:06:29.280
Jenna, I love this so much because you and I do our prep separately.
珍娜，我太喜欢这个了，因为你我都是分开做准备工作的。

1300
01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:31.620
So I don't know the rabbit holes you're going down.
所以我不知道你都钻研了些什么。

1301
01:06:31.820 --> 01:06:32.580
You're doing that.
你正在做那件事。

1302
01:06:32.740 --> 01:06:35.980
And I spent half an hour looking up different flavors of jelly beans.
我花了半小时去查各种不同口味的软糖。

1303
01:06:38.360 --> 01:06:39.380
It's all valid.
这都很有意义。

1304
01:06:39.380 --> 01:06:40.000
It's all good.
这都很好。

1305
01:06:40.080 --> 01:06:40.900
All important.
都很重要。

1306
01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:42.200
It's all important.
这都很重要。

1307
01:06:42.200 --> 01:06:44.900
Well, you know, so we go back to the luau.
嗯，你知道的，所以我们回到那个夏威夷派对上。

1308
01:06:45.180 --> 01:06:46.240
It's not going great.
情况不太妙。

1309
01:06:46.440 --> 01:06:48.800
People look like they're having a pretty lame time.
大家看起来都玩得很无聊。

1310
01:06:48.800 --> 01:06:53.540
And there's a scene between Roy and Pam and he kind of cheers her up.
然后罗伊和帕姆之间有一场戏，他算是把她逗乐了。

1311
01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:55.240
Yeah.
是啊。

1312
01:06:55.560 --> 01:06:55.820
Yeah.
没错。

1313
01:06:56.040 --> 01:06:58.820
Roy says, remember how we were deciding where to go on the honeymoon?
罗伊说，还记得我们当时在商量去哪儿度蜜月吗？

1314
01:06:58.820 --> 01:06:59.780
You wanted Hawaii.
你想要去夏威夷。

1315
